{
 "experiment": "e2 - re-rank P2Rank's own pockets to pick the peptide site",
 "question": "P2Rank offers a pocket touching the true interface in 78.6% of peptide complexes but ranks the wrong one first. Can the right one be picked without the answer?",
 "ground_truth": "receptor residues within 4.5 A (heavy atom) of any binder heavy atom, read from data/raw/epitope_gate/prepared.json as written by scripts/epitope_gate.py - not recomputed here",
 "scoring": "Jaccard over residue-number sets, identical to m2_gate.score",
 "verdict": "DO NOT RE-RANK - the headroom is real but these descriptors cannot reach it. The rule chosen on 40 development complexes moves held-out peptide Jaccard +0.0235 (0.1825 -> 0.2060, n=30, Wilcoxon p=0.465), which is 14% of the 0.1668 oracle headroom on those same complexes, against a +0.0834 bar. Running the folds the other way round moves it -0.0072, i.e. the wrong way. The mechanism is measurable: inside one protein the best descriptor of the 31 tried separates the right pocket from the wrong ones at AUC 0.651 [0.549, 0.738] (polar_fraction, 152 pairs over 47 complexes), and even a rule FIT AND SCORED on all 70 peptides - an upper bound, not a result - closes only 30%. The +0.128 gap is a fact about P2Rank's ranking; it is not recoverable from pocket geometry, P2Rank's own scores, or lining-residue composition.",
 "pass_criterion": {
  "stated": "held-out peptide Jaccard >= P2Rank top-1 + half the oracle headroom on the SAME held-out complexes, and paired Wilcoxon p < 0.05",
  "threshold_jaccard": 0.26592713250819106,
  "achieved_jaccard": 0.20601451359169642,
  "p_wilcoxon": 0.4652088184521418,
  "passed": false
 },
